@*
    Where the interview stands, and what is still coming.

    The rail is the whole shape of the process and it is on the screen from the first question, lit at
    the step in hand. An interview that shows only the question is a black box: the client cannot tell
    whether they are two minutes from the end or twenty, and the one thing every wizard owes them is
    the answer to that. So every step is visible, and the ones not reached are dim rather than absent.

    Its shape is the process's shape. Two steps happen once — the map is drawn, and at the end the
    finished domain is read and taken away — and everything between them happens again for every entry
    of that map, so those six are drawn inside a lap: bordered, stacked like sheets while entries
    remain, and headed by the entry it is a lap of.

    Under it, the entries of the map appear as they are named, in the client's own words.

    Nothing here explains itself in prose, and nothing here restates the question. A line under the
    rail saying what the step is after was tried and removed twice over: the question that opens a
    step already says it, in the model's own words and about this client's own work, so the line was
    either the same fact in a second wording — which reads as two facts to reconcile — or, once it had
    been made short enough not to be, a sentence true of any interview about any business.

    The rail and the entries are two kinds of thing — the shape of the work, and what the domain
    holds — and the eye tells them apart without being told.
*@

<div class="journey">

    @*
        Two steps on the line and six inside the lap, because that is the shape of the process: the
        map is drawn once and the finished domain is read once, and everything between them happens
        again for every entry of that map. A flat row of eight would say the opposite, and would have
        the light jump backwards from the last step to the second every time an agent was accepted.
    *@
    <ol class="stations">
        @Station(0)

        <li class="lap @(Remaining > 0 ? "stacked" : null)">
            <span class="lap-head">@LapHead</span>

            <ol class="lap-steps">
                @Station(1)
                @Station(2)
                @Station(3)
                @Station(4)
                @Station(5)
                @Station(6)
            </ol>
        </li>

        @Station(7)
    </ol>

    @*
        The map, and the entry being written is a tag that opens. What has been written down about an
        agent belongs to that agent, and the agent is already named here, lit among its neighbours —
        so this tag is the trigger and the panel drops out of it. Kept at the foot of the page instead,
        under its own copy of the same name, it was two objects where there is one thing, in the least
        looked-at place on the screen. The eye does not go down there, and nothing in a wizard should
        need looking for.
    *@
    @if (Entries.Any())
    {
        <div class="domain">
            <span class="what">@Caption</span>

            <div class="kinds">
                @foreach ((IntentDraft intent, string standing) in Entries)
                {
                    if (standing == "under-way")
                    {
                        <button class="under-way opens @(Showing ? "open" : null)" @onclick="() => Showing = !Showing"
                                title="What has been written down about this one so far">
                            @Named(intent)
                            <span class="counted">@AgentRows.Written(Rows) of @Rows.Count</span>
                        </button>
                    }
                    else
                    {
                        <span class="@standing">@Named(intent)</span>
                    }
                }
            </div>

            @if (Showing)
            {
                <AgentSoFar State="State" />
            }
        </div>
    }

</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The interview being reported on.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public InterviewState State { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>
    /// What the domain held before this interview, shown first and never lit.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public IEnumerable<IntentDraft> Established { get; set; } = [];

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the agent in hand is finished and waiting for the client's word.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Written { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The whole interview, in the client's words: the map once, then the lap every entry of it goes
    /// through, then the finished domain.
    /// </summary>
    // Each names its phase and nothing else, in one register: a rail is read at a glance, and a step
    // whose name is a whole clause is read as a sentence the eye has to finish. What the client is
    // actually being asked for is a different job and belongs to a different line — the one under the
    // rail, which names the entry and says in their own words what this phase wants and what it does
    // not want yet. Names above, meaning below: neither has to do both.
    //
    // The last of the lap is not a pass at all but the moment the client lets the agent into the
    // domain. It is inside the lap because that is what it is: the close of one entry's turn, after
    // which the lap starts again on the next. The last step is outside because it is the only other
    // thing that happens once — the whole domain, read and taken away, which is the page the client
    // lands on when the map is spent.
    private static readonly string[] Steps =
    [
        "Domain Exploration",
        /*Agent Modeling*/ "Target",
        /*Agent Modeling*/ "Personality",
        /*Agent Modeling*/ "Toolkit",
        /*Agent Modeling*/ "Instructions",
        /*Agent Modeling*/ "Formatting",
        "Agent Acceptance",
        "Domain Finalization"
    ];

    /// <summary>
    /// What the lap is a lap of, on its own edge.
    /// </summary>
    // The run is named once, on the border that encloses it, rather than three times inside it. A
    // prefix repeated on every station buries the part that tells them apart — which is the whole of
    // what a rail is read for — at the end of the line, where the eye arrives last.
    private const string Lap = "Agent Modeling";

    /// <summary>
    /// One step of the rail, wherever it sits.
    /// </summary>
    // The marks carry no number: two of them are outside the lap and six are inside it, so a count
    // running 1 to 8 would be claiming an order that only two of the steps actually have.
    private RenderFragment Station(int index) =>@<li class="station @Standing(index)">
        <span class="mark"></span>
        <span class="name">@Steps[index]</span>
    </li>;

    /// <summary>
    /// What the lap is a lap of: the entry being written and how far along the map it is, or what is
    /// about to start once the map exists.
    /// </summary>
    // Before the map exists the run has nothing to be a run of, and the head says only what it is.
    // Saying that it will come round for each entry would be describing a list the client is still
    // dictating, in the one moment they are deciding what goes on it.
    //
    // No count either. The strip under the rail is the map itself, with this entry lit among the
    // others, so a number here says in arithmetic what the entries say in the client's own words —
    // and a bare "1 of 4" a finger's width from four steps in a row invites being read as the steps.
    private string LapHead => About is { } about ? $"{Lap} · {about}" : Lap;

    /// <summary>
    /// How many entries of the map still have no agent, the one in hand excluded. The lap is drawn as
    /// a stack while any remain, which is what says it comes round again.
    /// </summary>
    private int Remaining => Drawing || State.At < 0 ? 1 : Math.Max(0, State.Map.Count - State.At - 1);

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the map is still being drawn, in which case no entry is being written yet.
    /// </summary>
    private bool Drawing => State.Pass == InterviewPass.DomainMapper;

    /// <summary>
    /// What the strip of entries is: the first step's own material.
    /// </summary>
    // Said plainly rather than repeating the rail's name for the phase: the rail is a label and this
    // is a heading over the list itself. How far along the map the work is belongs to the lap's own
    // head, which is where the entry being written is named — here it would be the same fact twice,
    // once under a list where the entries already say it by being lit.
    private const string Caption = "the work you hand over";

    /// <summary>
    /// Which entry the lap in hand is a lap of, or <c>null</c> while the map is being drawn and it is
    /// about to be a lap of all of them.
    /// </summary>
    private string? About =>
        Drawing || State.At < 0 || State.At >= State.Map.Count ? null : Named(State.Map[State.At]);

    /// <summary>
    /// Every entry the client can see, with how it stands: what earlier sittings left, then today's
    /// map — written, being written, still ahead.
    /// </summary>
    private IEnumerable<(IntentDraft Intent, string Standing)> Entries =>
        Established.Select(i => (i, "already"))
            .Concat(State.Map.Select((intent, index) =>
                (intent, index < State.At ? "already" : index == State.At ? "under-way" : "ahead")));

    /// <summary>
    /// The client's own word for an entry: its button once it has one, its name until then.
    /// </summary>
    private static string Named(IntentDraft intent) => intent.Label ?? intent.Name ?? "…";

    /// <summary>
    /// Where the rail is lit.
    /// </summary>
    // The pass is the index by construction: the enum and this list are in the same order, the map
    // included. Acceptance is the exception — no pass conducts it, and the interview stands there only
    // once everything is settled and the agent is waiting for the client's word. The last step is
    // never lit here: reaching it means leaving this page for the finished domain.
    private string Standing(int index)
    {
        int here = Written ? Accepting : (int)State.Pass;

        return index == here ? "here" : index < here ? "done" : string.Empty;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Where acceptance sits on the rail: the last step of the lap.
    /// </summary>
    private const int Accepting = 6;

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the rows behind the entry's tag are open.
    /// </summary>
    // Held here rather than in the panel, because the thing that opens is the tag and the tag is
    // drawn here. It survives a turn: a client who opened it to watch the agent fill in did not ask
    // to have it shut again every time they answer.
    private bool Showing { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// What has been written down about the agent in hand: the count on the tag, and the panel's own
    /// rows, from the one projection so the two cannot disagree.
    /// </summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<AgentRows.Row> Rows => AgentRows.Of(State);
}
